<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Adolf Hitler</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1889">1889</a>–<a href="/years/1945">1945</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Adolf Hitler was the leader of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nazi-party">Nazi Party</a> and dictator of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a> from 1933 to 1945. Appointed chancellor of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/weimar-republic">Weimar Republic</a> in January 1933, he quickly abolished democracy and built a totalitarian state. His expansionist wars began the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-world-war">Second World War</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a> in 1939, and his regime carried out the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/holocaust">Holocaust</a>. He killed himself in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/berlin">Berlin</a> in April 1945.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/adolf-hitler.jpg" alt="Adolf Hitler" width="320" height="434" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Heinrich Hoffmann · CC BY-SA 3.0 de · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Hitler_portrait_crop_(cropped)(2).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Its greatest political export was Fascism: Mussolini’s regime, with its mass party, leader cult and corporatist claims, became a template for movements across Europe and influenced <b>Adolf Hitler</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-italy">Kingdom of Italy · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Adolf Hitler</b> ordered the destruction of Yugoslavia, and on 6 April 1941 German aircraft bombed Belgrade, killing thousands, while German, Italian and Hungarian forces invaded.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-yugoslavia">Kingdom of Yugoslavia · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Hitler</b> never formally abolished the Weimar constitution: the Enabling Act of 1933 was renewed by the Reichstag in 1937 and 1939 and extended by decree in 1943.</blockquote><a href="/empire/nazi-germany">Nazi Germany · At a glance</a></li><li><blockquote>…had established personal and totalitarian rule by 1929; Benito Mussolini came to power in Italy in 1922 and from 1925 built the first fascist dictatorship; and <b>Adolf Hitler</b>, appointed chancellor in January 1933, used emergency decrees and the Enabling Act of 1933 to destroy German democracy within months.</blockquote><a href="/government/dictatorship">Dictatorship · Through History</a></li><li><blockquote>Fascist regimes differed: in Benito Mussolini’s Italy the party was subordinated to the state, and in <b>Adolf Hitler</b>’s Germany party and state agencies competed under a leader who stood above both.</blockquote><a href="/government/one-party-state">One-party state · How It Works</a></li><li><blockquote>Benito Mussolini was appointed prime minister of Italy in 1922 and had made himself dictator by 1926; in Germany, after chancellors had governed for three years by presidential decree, the Reichstag passed the Enabling Act in March 1933, letting <b>Adolf Hitler</b>’s government make laws without it.</blockquote><a href="/government/parliamentary-democracy">Parliamentary democracy · Through History</a></li></ul></section>
